    "md5": "2b90e53f4fa3d40bca47df2baea1100d",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1866,
    "chunk_text": "Now, Sir, let us look at what the situation is globally. There have been some global developments. Globally, mediation is increasingly used for the resolution of many categories of disputes including commercial disputes, intellectual property disputes, family disputes and investor-State disputes, and many countries have enacted mediation legislation because of the benefits it offers.\n\nIf we take the UN Mediation Convention, because mediation has been extensively used to settle trade and business disputes across the borders, the United Nations adopted the Convention on International Settlement Agreements Resulting from Mediation in 2018 to provide a uniform framework for the enforcement for international mediated settlement agreements. The UN Mediation Convention is on mediation and the UN New York Arbitration Convention is on arbitration. Sri Lanka\n\n489 490\n\n[\u0d9c\u0dbb\u0dd4 \u0dd3\u0dad\u0dd2\u0da5 \u0dc4\u0dbb\u0dca\u0dc2\u0dab \u0db1\u0dcf\u0db1\u0dcf\u0db8\u0d9a\u0dca\u0d9a\u0dcf\u0dbb \u0db8 \u0dba\u0dc4\u0dad\u0dca\u200d\u0dcf]\n\n2026 \u0da2\u0dd6\u0db1\u0dd2 11\n\nsigned that Convention in 2019 and enacted the Recognition and Enforcement of International Settlement Agreements Resulting from Mediation Act, No. 5 of 2024 in January, 2024, ratifying it. Sri Lanka was the 14th country to ratify the Convention and as at now, the number of ratifications has increased to 22.\n\nUNCITRAL, which is the dedicated body established by the United Nations to work on harmonizing trade laws across the globe and which drafted the text for the UN Convention, has also formulated a model law to govern international mediations and their enforcement and also has its own Mediation Rules as well as Guidelines for the conduct of mediations.\n\nThe International Centre for Settlement of Investment Disputes - ICSID - of the World Bank Group, too, established its Mediation Rules in 2022, specially designed for the resolution of investment-related disputes."
  },
  {
