    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "4d03cc4e9327a53d1235ab6b3684fdd5",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1919,
    "chunk_text": "Further, Sir, it would also reduce the cost for litigants because we know that in Sri Lanka, the cost of litigation is very high. If a particular person has an issue and he decides to go for mediation, it would definitely reduce the cost of litigation. So, overall, I would say this is a very\n\npositive move as it not only encourages the use of mediation, but also inspires trust in the process and in those who administer it.\n\nFurther, Hon. Minister, I would like to draw your attention to Clause 5 of the Bill, which statutorily bars the court from entertaining a dispute merely because mediation has commenced, even in the absence of a mediation agreement. It forces parties to stay out of court until mediation certifies non-settlement, and that could turn mediation into a procedural deadlock. So, basically, this requirement of the non-settlement certificate could be used by parties to delay the whole dispute resolution process. In particular, you could have a litigant who, in bad faith, wants to delay the resolving of a particular dispute, especially in the case of unlawful rent and so on and so forth. So, that could definitely be taken on by such a party. For example, Hon. Minister, under Singapore's Mediation Act of 2017, the courts can only stay proceedings when there is a formal agreement. But, like I said before, in Sri Lanka, even in the absence of a formal agreement, if one party wants to go into mediation, it could do so and, in that event, the other party cannot go into litigation. So, that kind of act, basically, goes against the concept of mediation.\n\nThe other thing is the requirement of a Mediation Service Provider. That essentially means that if the parties wish to obtain the services of a mediator who is well-versed in a particular industry, they are precluded from doing so because they have to go with a registered service provider, which could create some sort of bureaucracy."
  },
