    "md5": "c98f8058ca8071e6f2b5d4d65e6935bb",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1910,
    "chunk_text": "What is happening in the Supreme Court today? Even when constituting benches, the practice of the Supreme Court has been to nominate the seniormost Judges to those. But, that is not happening today. A convention that had been followed in the past has completely been abandoned. Is it to do with the new attempt to increase the retirement age of the judges? It has been the demand of this House that the pending vacancies in the Court of Appeal and the Supreme Court be immediately filled. The President is bound to nominate the judges according to their seniority to the relevant vacancies. High Court Judges, before completing 60 years of age, could be sent up to the Court of Appeal. There is a seniority list in the Judicial Service Commission. The President is duty-bound to nominate those judges, if they are below the age of 60 years, to be promoted to the Court of Appeal. And from the Court of Appeal, those judges who have not reached the age of 63 years have to be promoted to the Supreme Court. But, what is happening today? Today, this serious matter has a major impact on the delivery of justice in this country, and that has been repeatedly pointed out.\n\nmonths from the 27th of February to the 27th of September before completing his term. Thereafter, there will be a lady Chief Justice, Justice B.V. Nagarathna. She will be there as the Chief Justice only for 36 days. So, they promote people according to their seniority; there is no picking and choosing by the President. And, it must also be pointed out that consulting the Chief Justice happens only when it comes to the Constitutional Council. It is the Constitutional Council which has to routinely get the opinion of the Chief Justice about the suitability of the candidates. Now, what is happening invariably is, there is serious interference with the Judiciary and its functions by holding on and not making those appointments in time."
