    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "25f5e67dc12a44193273ba0a42c76b08",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2034,
    "chunk_text": "\u2022 Increasing marital separation and divorce.\n\nb. Economic pressures\n\n\u2022 Rising cost of living.\n\n\u2022 Increased housing, education and childcare expenses.\n\n\u2022 Economic uncertainty and reduced real\n\nhousehold income.\n\nbecome increasingly expensive.\n\nc. Impact of the COVID-19 pandemic\n\n\u2022 Delayed family formation and childbearing decisions.\n\n\u2022 Social, economic and health-related\n\nuncertainties.\n\nd. Migration\n\n\u2022 Large-scale migration of young adults\n\nand couples in reproductive age groups.\n\n\u2022 Temporary or long-term separation of\n\nspouses due to overseas employment.\n\ne. Social and cultural changes\n\n\u2022 Preference for smaller families.\n\n\u2022 Increased female participation in higher\n\neducation and employment.\n\n\u2022 Changing attitudes regarding family size\n\nand timing of childbirth.\n\nf. Reproductive health factors\n\n\u2022 Increased use of family planning methods.\n\n\u2022 Delayed first pregnancies.\n\n\u2022 Possible contribution of subfertility and\n\ninfertility, although further research is required to quantify this effect.\n\ng. Emerging environmental concerns\n\nInternational evidence suggests that climate change and concerns regarding future living conditions may influence reproductive decisions among younger generations.\n\nThe observed decline is, therefore, likely to be the combined effect of several long-term structural changes rather than a single identifiable cause.\n\n(ii) From a demographic perspective, the key indicator is the Total Fertility Rate - TFR - rather than the absolute number of births.\n\nDemographically, the optimum fertility level required to sustain the population on the long-term is the replacement-level fertility rate of approximately 2.1 children per woman. At the current population size and age structure of Sri Lanka, this would correspond to approximately 330,000 -360,000 live births per year.\n\nSustained fertility below replacement level may result in population ageing, a shrinking workforce and increasing dependency ratios over time.\n\n(iii) This needs to be addressed at the policy level."
  },
