    "chunk_size_bytes": 1798,
    "chunk_text": "Hon. Minister, since there is a 350-word count to adhere to with regard to this particular Question, I could not go into detail. I raise this Question because, as you are also aware, in 1996, during the Krishanthi Kumaraswamy excavation, around 15 skeletal remains were sent to Scotland, out of which two were identified. The balance 13 skeletal remains not unidentified remain at the University of Lancashire. So, their custodian is the University of Lancashire, I believe. If you are really serious about identifying them really fast, all that the Government needs to do is to write a letter and ask, \"Could you give us expertise?\" I have just concluded a\n\n1249 1250\n\n2026 \u0da2\u0dd4\u0db1\u0dd2 25\n\n(f) What steps have been taken to review evidence and material connected to the original Chemmani investigations that arose following the Krishanthi Kumaraswamy case, including any remains, samples or forensic reports previously sent to Scotland or elsewhere overseas and what is their current status?\n\nThat is the information I gave just before raising this question.\n\n(g) At a lawyers\u2019 forum held in Jaffna last week, the Hon. Minister reportedly stated - I read it on the news that you attended a meeting comprised of lawyers - that steps would be taken to advance the investigation to the next stage of identifying the recovered remains. Will the Hon. Minister, therefore, assure Parliament that a reputed and internationally experienced forensic institution will be engaged to assist in identifying the victims?\n\nI hope, Hon. Minister, that you observed the emphasis I put on a \u201creputed and internationally experienced forensic institution\u201d.\n\n(h) What further action has the Government taken to establish the truth regarding those buried at Chemmani and provide answers to the affected families?"
  },
