    "chunk_size_bytes": 1877,
    "chunk_text": "(d) The possible cause of death including if the victim has died due to a crime will have to be identified through a forensic anthropological analysis. Ascertaining the period of death takes place through the analyses by the archaeologists and the Judicial Medical Officers. Details will be available when they submit the respective reports.\n\n(e) The Government has invited forensic assistance\n\nfrom international entities, recognizing their experience and expertise in mass grave investigations and is in negotiation with different embassies. The Office on Missing Persons, as an independent State mechanism, also engages with different foreign entities and is closely working with the ICRC, among others. For example, on the 16th October, 2025, the OMP collaborated with the ICRC to invite two experts to conduct an age analysis of sub-adults training for Judicial Medical Officers, Odontologists, and Anatomists at the University of Jaffna in order to assist with Chemmani and other mass grave cases. Similarly, the Office on Missing Persons will take measures based on the requirements arisen.\n\nI can assure you that we have spoken to embassies, made all the local finances that are necessary for the excavation, and now, we are in the excavation and exhumation period. But, when it comes to DNA analysis, depending on the type and the nature, we will definitely have to go for, as you said, internationally reputed and recognized institutions because some of the expertise may not be available here. Therefore, it will be done, but depending on the requirements. And, those requirements are informed to us by the anthropological team led by Prof. Raj Somadeva and the courts.\n\n(f) You referred to the Krishanthi Kumaraswamy case\n\nand said that some remains had been sent to Scotland. Now, that matter, of which the OMP has no information and I did not have sufficient time"
