    "md5": "496da8c1c6dc8d839dabfb7939c27132",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1818,
    "chunk_text": "I can assure you that we have spoken to embassies, made all the local finances that are necessary for the excavation, and now, we are in the excavation and exhumation period. But, when it comes to DNA analysis, depending on the type and the nature, we will definitely have to go for, as you said, internationally reputed and recognized institutions because some of the expertise may not be available here. Therefore, it will be done, but depending on the requirements. And, those requirements are informed to us by the anthropological team led by Prof. Raj Somadeva and the courts.\n\n(f) You referred to the Krishanthi Kumaraswamy case\n\nand said that some remains had been sent to Scotland. Now, that matter, of which the OMP has no information and I did not have sufficient time\n\n(g) Then, you asked about the last conference held in\n\nJaffna. Actually, I met the official Bar there. I was very honoured to have Mr. Sumanthiran also seated with me till the end of it. Yes, they also did inquire about Chemmani. Actually, what I learnt when I went to the Chemmani mass graves, Hon. Member, and spoke with Prof. Raj Somadeva and the team was that, now, they are in the excavation and exhumation stage and that there is another stage we have to go to - processing. So, what I did ask them was, without waiting for the complete excavation to be over, whether it is possible to start the second and the third stages, for which they could not give me an answer then and there. But, they - the JMOs and the anthropologists, including the leader of the team, Prof. Raj Somadeva - agreed to have discussions. What I inquired was, without waiting till one stage is over, whether we could go for the other stages. So, that will be discussed; in fact, I am the one who suggested that and they said they are willing to discuss that."
